  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through three innings until Tampa's six-run fourth flattened everything.",
    "what_this_means": "They had chances in the early innings. A runner in scoring position in the third, another in the fifth, two more in the seventh. And converted none of them. Eight strikeouts. Five hits. That gap between activity and production is not a slump; it is a closed door. Jeffrey Springs lasted 3.1 innings and got out, which meant the bullpen had to cover 5.2. Hartlieb and Roycroft and Tur held the damage there, which matters because without them this would have been worse than 10-1. But that's the point: the pitching gave them the game. The offense left it on the field. Lawrence Butler hit one solo shot for a moment of oxygen that went nowhere. The rest of the lineup logged eight strikeouts and touched nothing that cost runs. They never led. They never tied it after the fourth. The game decided itself before most games get interesting.",
    "what_to_watch": "Daniel Espino is bringing a 2.92 ERA into tonight against a lineup that struck out eight times on two hits yesterday. The kind of matchup where discipline matters more than hope.",
